{"name":"Meander","hex":"#8f8c79","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Meander","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A town in Meander Valley council area, northern Tasmania, Australia.","A river in northern Tasmania, which joins the South Esk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Meander"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meander","blocks":[{"text":"A meander is one of a series of regular sinuous curves in the channel of a stream. It is produced as a watercourse erodes the sediments of an outer, concave bank and deposits sediments on an inner, convex bank which is typically a point bar. The result of this coupled erosion and sedimentation is the formation of a sinuous course as the channel migrates back and forth across the axis of a floodplain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Meander-en.svg/330px-Meander-en.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meander","wikidata":"Q180537"},{"title":"Meander (art)","blocks":[{"text":"A meander or meandros is a decorative border constructed from a continuous line, shaped into a repeated motif. To some Italians, these patterns are known as \"Greek lines\". Such a design may also be called the Greek fret or Greek key design, although these terms are modern designations; this decorative motif appears much earlier and among Near and Far Eastern cultures that are far from Greece. Usually the term is used for motifs with straight lines and right angles and the many versions with rounded shapes are called running scrolls or, following the etymological origin of the term, may be identified as water wave motifs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Greek_key_on_a_stove_in_the_in_the_D.A._Sturdza_House%2C_in_Bucharest.jpg/330px-Greek_key_on_a_stove_in_the_in_the_D.A._Sturdza_House%2C_in_Bucharest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meander_(art)","wikidata":"Q898879"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG/500px-Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG/1280px-Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG/500px-Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meanders of the Rio Cauto at Guamo Embarcadero, Cuba","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARio-cauto-cuba.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg/500px-Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg/1280px-Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg/500px-Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Jordan River, near the Dead Sea, 1937","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Bend2.png/500px-Bend2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Bend2.png/500px-Bend2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Straight channel culmi